    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "relatives",
        "title": "Who, which: -ye-, -o-",
        "body": [
          "To say “who” or “which”, Swahili puts a small marker inside the verb, after the tense: **a-li-ye-kuja** (he or she who came), **wa-li-o-kuja** (those who came).",
          "The marker agrees with the noun: **-ye-** for one person, **-o-** for many, **-cho-** for a ki- thing: **kitabu nilichonunua** (the book I bought)."
        ],
        "table": [
          ["Noun", "who/which came"],
          ["mtu", "mtu aliyekuja"],
          ["watu", "watu waliokuja"],
          ["kitabu", "kitabu kilichokuja"],
          ["barua", "barua iliyokuja"]
        ],
        "targetColumns": [0, 1]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Weddings",
        "body": [
          "A coastal wedding can last several days, with women’s celebrations, henna and **taarab** music. Guests are counted in hundreds, and everyone who comes is fed."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Holidays for all",
        "body": [
          "Kenya and Tanzania keep both Christian and Muslim holidays. Neighbours greet each other with **Sikukuu njema!** at Eid and at Christmas alike, and share food."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
